Note that I'm not ridiculing anyone (except the buffoon who
sits in his armchair and sputters "utter nonsense" everytime
someone says something that he doesn't like.)  I'm just
pointing out that different languages have different
strengths.  English is flexible and it's flexible in lots
of different ways.  This makes it a very good, maybe the
best ever, candidate for a Lingua Franca.  When I walk across
a large campus (Say Univ of Texas at Austin or Univ. of
Calgary) on _any_ given day, I overhear a conversation between
two foreign students from widely different countries, with very thick 
accents which I can barely make out, communicating in English.
(E.g., A Pakistani and a Chinese.)  One can speak English very
badly and still be understood.  One can screw up the pronounciation
AND the word order AND the conjugation AND verb agreement AND
the gender all the same time, and the hearer still knows what
one meant.  

It's a plain fact that English has this strength.  It's up
for debate whether English is the very best at this.  

Further, it's up for debate how much this strength played
a role in Britain's conquest of the world.  Certainly it
had some effect.  We could argue about how much.


 
